RacistExterminator (to Sarah_White22):

 Sure, I can help with that.

Generative models and discriminative models are two different approaches to machine learning. The main difference between them is how they approach the problem of learning to make predictions.

Generative models try to learn the underlying distribution of the data and use it to make predictions. They aim to learn the joint probability distribution of the inputs and outputs, and then use this distribution to make predictions. Generative models are often used for tasks like image generation, speech recognition, and natural language processing.

Discriminative models, on the other hand, try to learn a function that maps inputs to outputs. They do not try to learn the underlying distribution of the data, but instead learn to make a prediction directly from the inputs. Discriminative models are often used for tasks like image classification, speech recognition, and natural language processing.

In summary, generative models learn the underlying distribution of the data and make predictions based on that, while discriminative models learn a function that maps inputs to outputs directly.

